s inverse zero-sum problems in [6, 19]. ...   * r=2 and n=2 k 3 ℓ p by Theorem 1.2(iii), where p is a prime and k,ℓ≥0 are integers,\\n## 1 Introduction\\n\\ni∈I∑​a i​≡0(mod n).\\n\\nNote that the condition in (1.1) depends only on residues modulo n and does not determine whether the selected sum also vanishes modulo n 2. For prime moduli, Gao et al. [7] studied exact divisibility conditions for zero-sum subsequences. For a prime p, the p-adic valuation of an integer x is v p​(x)=sup{k∈Z:\\\\penalty k≥0 and p k∣x}. For r=1 and n=p, their constant s(p,1) coincides with the least length such that every integer sequence of that length whose terms are not divisible by p contains a p-term subsequence whose sum has p-adic valuation exactly 1. Equivalently, the selected sum must vanish modulo p but not modulo p 2. Recently, it was shown in [8] that s(p,1)≤3 p−2 for every prime p, improving the bound s(p,1)≤s(Z p 2​)=4 p−3 obtained from Kemnitz’s theorem [11, 18].\\n\\ni∈I∑​a i​≡0(mod n),but i∈I∑​a i​≡0(mod n 2).\\n\\nBy definition, we immediately have w r​(n)≤s r​(n) and s 1​(p)=s(p,1). For n>2, Sun proved that 2 r n+1≤s r​(n)≤n(n r−1)+1 in [20, Theorem 1.3]. For r=2, Sun conjectured in the same paper that w 2​(n)=4 n−3 for every n>1 in Conjecture 1.1(ii) and s 2​(n)=4 n+1 for every n>2 in Conjecture 1.2. In Remark 1.2, he also asked whether w r​(n)=s(Z n r​) for all integers n>1 and r>0. More related constants and conjectures can also be found in [7, 20].\\n\\nThis paper aims to study the zero-sum problems of new types and to answer Sun’s conjectures and questions listed above. In the following, we call an admissible sequence bad if no n-term subsequence satisfies (1.2), and w-bad if no nonempty subsequence does. Such sequences give lower bounds for s r​(n) and w r​(n), respectively. We write ∣A∣ for the length of A, σ(A) for the sum of its terms in the ambient abelian group, and v[m] for m copies of v. In addition, sequence products mean concatenation and an empty product denotes the empty sequence.\\n\\nOur first main result addresses Sun’s fixed-length conjecture through a construction from lattice polytopes.\\n\\nA r,n​=v∈V r​∏​v[n−1],\\\\penalty with\\\\penalty V r​={±1 J​:\\\\penalty ∅=J⊆[r]}\\\\penalty and\\\\penalty 1 J​=j∈J∑​e j​\\n\\n  * In [20, Theorem 1.3(ii)], Sun proved s r​(n)≥2 r n+1 for r≥1 and n>2. Our bound is strictly stronger for r≥2 and n>2, except (r,n)=(2,3), where the bounds coincide. For r=2 and n≥4, the bound (1.4) gives\\n  * For r≥2, Sun also proved 3⋅2 r+1≤s r​(3)≤2⋅3 r−1 in [20, Theorem 1.4(i)]. Our bound s r​(3)≥4⋅2 r−3 improves the lower bound by 2 r−4, with strict improvement for r≥3.\\n\\nOur second main result presents some exact values for w r​(n) and solves Sun’s rank-two conjecture for certain moduli, which derives from a complete classification of all w-bad sequences of arbitrary lengths for n=2 and r≥1, and a complete classification of all w-bad sequences of length 4 p−4 modulo p 2 for n=p and r=2. During the process, we also obtain doubling and tripling inequalities to propagate the result w 2​(t) to w 2​(d t) for d∈{2,3}.\\n\\n  1. For every integer r≥1, we have w r​(2)=2 r+1.\\n  2. For d∈{2,3} and every integer t>1, we have w 2​(d t)≤d w 2​(t)+3(d−1).\\n  3. For every prime p and integers k,ℓ≥0, we have w 2​(2 k 3 ℓ p)=4⋅2 k 3 ℓ p−3.\\n\\n  * Since s(Z 2 r​)=2 r+1 [9], Theorem 1.2(i) answers this question negatively for every r≥3.\\n  * Since s(Z n 2​)=4 n−3 for every n≥2 by [11, 18], Theorem 1.2(iii) answers this question affirmatively in rank two for these moduli.\\n\\nOur third main result shows that this failure of the equality in Sun’s question, as stated in Remark 1.2, is not confined to the binary case.\\n\\nw r​(n)≤n⌈r lo g 2​n⌉(⌈r lo g 2​n⌉+1)+1,\\n\\nTheorem 1.3 answers Sun’s equality question [20, Remark 1.2] negatively for every fixed modulus n and all sufficiently large ranks r. In particular, the limit strengthens this conclusion to an asymptotic separation between w r​(n) and the classical Erdős-Ginzburg-Ziv constant s(Z n r​).\\n\\nThe rest of the paper is organized as follows. Section 2 proves Theorem 1.1. Section 3 gives the binary and prime-modulus classifications and proves Theorem 1.2. Section 4 proves Theorem 1.3.\\n\\nNote also that an interior point cannot be a vertex in a lattice polytope of positive dimension.\\n\\nA(P)=v∈Vert(P)∏​v[n−1]\\n\\ni=1∑r​m i​b i​⟷(m 1​,…,m r​),\\\\penalty with\\\\penalty m i​∈Z.\\n\\nFor any vertex v, the conditions dim(P)=r≥1 and 0 interior imply v=0. Convexity and the interior condition also give ε>0 such that, for real t i​ with ∣t i​∣<ε for all i, we have n v​+(1−n 1​)∑i=1 r​t i​b i​∈P. In particular, taking t i​=0 for all i gives n v​∈P. As (1−n 1​)ε>0, the point n v​ is interior. If v belonged to n L, then n v​∈L. In this case, we deduce that 0=n v​∈P∩L would be an interior point, contrary to the assumption P∩L={0}∪Vert(P). Therefore, v∈/n L. This shows that A(P) is admissible.\\n\\nn x​=n 1​a i​+n n−1​​n−1 1​j=i∑​a j​​∈P,\\\\penalty and\\\\penalty n−1 1​j=i∑​a j​∈P.\\n\\nL r​=Z r and P r​=conv([0,1]r∪[−1,0]r)​\\n\\nP r​=conv({0,1}r∪{−1,0}r)=conv(V r​∪{0}).\\n\\nP r​∩L r​={0}∪V r​.\\n\\nℓ J​(x)=(r−k+1)i∈J∑​x i​−k i∈/J∑​x i​.\\n\\nThus, P r​ satisfies the hypotheses of Lemma 2.1, and A(P r​)=A r,n​. Since ∣V r​∣=2(2 r−1), the lemma yields the desired badness and the lower bound (1.4). ∎\\n\\nFor a polytope P as in Lemma 2.1, Nill’s bound says that ∣Vert(P)∣≤2 r+1−2 [15, Corollary 6.3]. Therefore, the polytope P r​ used in Theorem 1.1 reaches this bound and gives the largest lower bound on s r​(n) obtainable from this lemma for fixed r and n.\\n\\n## 3 Proof of Theorem 1.2\\n\\nThis section proves Theorem 1.2 in three parts. For some exact values of w r​(n) determined in this section, we need to classify all w-bad sequences of the corresponding lengths. Note also that analogous classification questions for classical zero-sum constants were studied as inverse zero-sum problems in [6, 19].\\n\\n### 3.1 A lower bound on wr​(n)w_{r}(n) and the proof of Theorem 1.2(i)\\n\\nWe first provide a unified lower bound on w r​(n) by constructing a w-bad sequence in Z r of length 2 r(n−1) as follows.\\n\\nE r,n​=j=1∏r​e j[n−1]​(−e j​)[n−1]\\n\\nBy construction, E r,n​ is admissible. For a subsequence, let u j​ and v j​ count the selected copies of e j​ and −e j​, respectively. Since 0≤u j​,v j​≤n−1, u j​−v j​ is divisible by n only when u j​=v j​. Therefore, every subsequence of E r,n​ with sum zero modulo n has sum zero in Z r, and E r,n​ is w-bad. Since ∣E r,n​∣=2 r(n−1), we obtain w r​(n)≥2 r(n−1)+1. ∎\\n\\nWe now prove Theorem 1.2(i) by showing that 2 r+1 is also an upper bound for w r​(2). For this purpose, the following theorem classifies all nonempty w-bad sequences in Z r for n=2, with the normal form specified modulo 4.\\n\\nA=x 1​⋯x d​y 1​⋯y k​,\\\\penalty with\\\\penalty y j​≡−i∈S j​∑​x i​(mod 4)\\\\penalty for\\\\penalty 1≤j≤k,\\n\\n0≡y j​+y ℓ​+i∈S j​△S ℓ​∑​x i​≡−i∈S j​∑​x i​−i∈S ℓ​∑​x i​+i∈S j​△S ℓ​∑​x i​=−2 i∈S j​∩S ℓ​∑​x i​(mod 4),\\n\\nσ(B)=j∈J∑​(y j​+i∈S j​∑​x i​)≡0(mod 4).\\n\\nUsing the disjointness and nonemptiness of the sets S j​ in Theorem 3.1, we obtain k≤d≤r and ∣A∣=d+k≤2 d≤2 r for every w-bad sequence A. It follows that w r​(2)≤2 r+1. On the other hand, taking n=2 in Lemma 3.1 yields w r​(2)≥2 r+1. We therefore conclude that w r​(2)=2 r+1 for every r≥1. ∎\\n\\n### 3.3 Proofs of Theorems 1.2(ii) and 1.2(iii)\\n\\nWe now prove Theorem 1.2(ii) and then combine it with Corollary 3.1 to obtain part (iii). For the part of the tripling inequality in Theorem 1.2(ii), we also need the following length bound and the structure of sequences attaining it.\\n\\nA≡U[2](−U)[2]V[2](−V)[2](U+V)[2](−U−V)[2](mod 9).\\n\\nS={a mod 3:\\\\penalty a is a term of A}⊆F 3 2​∖{0}.\\n\\nSuppose that four terms x 1​,…,x 4​ of A have the same residue u∈S modulo 3. Then ∑j=i​x j​≡0(mod 9) for 1≤i≤4. By subtraction, we have x 1​≡x 2​≡x 3​≡x 4​(mod 9) and hence, 3 x 1​≡0(mod 9), contrary to admissibility. Thus, each element of S is represented by at most three terms of A.\\n\\nS∩(−S)=∅,−a−b∈/S,\\\\penalty ∀\\\\penalty a,b∈S\\\\penalty with\\\\penalty a=b.\\n\\nWe now have that ∣S∣≥∣A∣/2≥6. Since the total number of one-dimensional subspaces of F 3 2​ is four, there are at least two one-dimensional subspaces of F 3 2​ that contain both nonzero elements in S. There are therefore terms U,V of A with linearly independent residues u=U mod 3 and v=V mod 3 such that {±u,±v}⊆S. Since ∣S∣≥6, at least one of ±u±v belongs to S. After changing the signs of u,v and choosing terms U,V with these residues, we may assume that u+v∈S.\\n\\nx mod 3=ε u x mod 3=ε v x mod 3=ε(u+v)​ ⟹ x≡ε U(mod 9), ⟹ x≡ε V(mod 9), ⟹ x≡ε(U+V)(mod 9).​\\n\\nIf ∣A∣=12, equality holds throughout, so ∣S∣=6 and each element of S is represented by exactly two terms of A. The congruences (3.9) give the stated representation, with det(U,V)≡0(mod 3). This completes the proof. ∎\\n\\nType I:Type II:​∣B∣=1,σ(B)≡0(mod d);σ(B)≡0(mod d),σ(B)≡0(mod d 2).​\\n\\nTo contradict (3.10), we now construct w 2​(t) pairwise disjoint nonempty subsequences of A, each of Type I or Type II. Let q denote the number of terms of A that are zero modulo d. These terms give q pairwise disjoint one-term subsequences of Type I, and hence, q<w 2​(t) by (3.10). Note that all the other ∣A∣−q=d w 2​(t)+3(d−1)−q terms of A are nonzero modulo d. We have two cases, depending on the value of d.\\n\\nCase 1: d=2. Among any seven remaining terms, three terms x,y,z have the same nonzero residue modulo 2. Since 2 x=(x+y)+(x+z)−(y+z)≡0(mod 4) cannot be allowed, at least one of the three pair sums x+y,x+z,y+z is nonzero modulo 4. This gives a two-term subsequence of Type II.\\n\\nq+(w 2​(t)−q−1)+1=w 2​(t)\\n\\nCase 2: d=3. By Corollary 3.1, we have w 2​(3)=9. After deleting the q terms of A that are zero modulo 3, there remain 3 w 2​(t)+6−q terms, all nonzero modulo 3. If w 2​(t)−q=1, the remaining length is 9+2 q≥9=w 2​(3) and these terms contain a subsequence of Type II. Together, they give q+1=w 2​(t) pairwise disjoint nonempty subsequences of A, each of Type I or Type II, contradicting (3.10).\\n\\nIf w 2​(t)−q≥2, the remaining length is at least 12+2 q. Whenever at least 13 terms remain, Lemma 3.7 gives a subsequence of Type II with at most three terms. By an argument similar to that in Case 1, we can select and remove w 2​(t)−q−2 such subsequences of Type II from the remaining terms, leaving at least 12+2 q terms. Let J consist of any 12 of these remaining terms.\\n\\nq+(w 2​(t)−q−2)+2=w 2​(t)\\n\\nAs a result, there is no admissible w-bad sequence of length d w 2​(t)+3(d−1) in Z 2 modulo d t, and hence, w 2​(d t)≤d w 2​(t)+3(d−1). This completes the proof of Theorem 1.2(ii). ∎\\n\\nw 2​(2 k 3 ℓ p)≤2 k 3 ℓ(w 2​(p)+3)−3=4⋅2 k 3 ℓ p−3.\\n\\nWe end this section with the following remark and question.\\n\\n  * r=1 and n>1 by Sun’s result [20, Theorem 1.1],\\n  * r≥1 and n=2 by Theorem 1.2(i), and\\n  * r=2 and n=2 k 3 ℓ p by Theorem 1.2(iii), where p is a prime and k,ℓ≥0 are integers,\\n\\nDoes w r​(n)=2 r(n−1)+1 hold for every r≥1 and n>1?\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nzero_sum_w_ge · AFTD (https://rainwangphy.github.io/aftd-platform/d/zero_sum_w_ge/)\\nciteturn2search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3 weeks ago; Crawled: yesterday;     arXiv:2610.19965 (Proofs and disproofs of some conjectures on zero-sum problems), Lemma 3.1 (consequence).\\n\\n# zero_sum_w_ge\\n\\ntheorem verified formalization\\n\\nw_r(n) ≥ 2r(n−1) + 1 for all r ≥ 1 and n > 1.\\n\\n## Statement\\n\\n[Button: Copy]\\n    \\n    theorem zero_sum_w_ge (r n : ℕ) (hn : 1 < n) : ((2 * r * (n - 1) + 1 : ℕ) : ℕ∞) ≤ zero_sum_w r n\\n\\nFormalizes\\n    arXiv:2610.19965 (Proofs and disproofs of some conjectures on zero-sum problems), Lemma 3.1 (consequence).\\nVerified\\n    15 Oct 2026\\nAxioms\\n    `Classical.choice``Quot.sound``propext`\\nBuilt on 6\\n 